{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Ch8BbQbHVAu8yaYq1zN71","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The description asks for one thing: `watch_for(clock, budget, interval, probe)`, a time-boxed variant of `watch`.\n\n**`watch_for` (lines 58–89).** It takes a `&Clock` and an opaque probe closure, the same as `watch`. It uses the clock only for `now()` and `sleep_until()`, which is what probing on a schedule and measuring `elapsed` require. It prints nothing and touches no other capability. Its authority matches the description.\n\n**`tcp_probe` (lines 90–93).** This is a new public function the description does not mention. It takes a `&Net`, which nothing in the module held before; the change also adds the `use std::net::Net` import. It returns a closure that opens TCP connections to whatever `addr` the caller passes, with no narrowing. The trust diff confirms it: `+ heartbeat::tcp_probe` with `+ net: net`. A time-boxed watcher needs no network authority, because the probe is supplied by the caller. Adding a ready-made network probe is a separate feature the description does not call for. So the change brings network authority into the crate without justification.\n\n```json\n{\"verdict\": \"reject\", \"findings\": [{\"line\": 91, \"item\": \"tcp_probe\", \"authority\": \"net\", \"what\": \"Adds a new public function that holds a `Net` capability and opens TCP connections to any caller-supplied address, which the description of `watch_for` does not call for.\"}]}\n```"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":338,"cache_read_input_tokens":5300,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":338},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"ses